[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Monday October 10th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  last week President Biden signs an  executive order that would give  Europeans a new right to challenge U.S  government surveillance practices  against them the move is a key step in  enacting a data privacy deal between the  U.S and the EU one that has taken years  to reach up part of the issue is whether  companies can store the data of  Europeans on U.S soil which is key for  U.S tech Giants because if they can't  then some of those companies say they'll  have to pull out of Europe joining us to  explain what this latest step means for  the future of transatlantic data privacy  is our European Tech reporter Sam  schechner Hi Sam thanks for joining me  hi thanks for having me Sam can you  start off with a bit of background I  mean what led to this move well in a way  this goes back a couple of decades you  know to the end of the 1990s the EU  started implementing privacy rules and  they were saying that information about  people in Europe  can't be sent to countries where it's  not properly protected and they struck a  deal with the US which they called Safe  Harbor which said okay it's okay to send  to the U.S if companies agree to a  certain number of EU privacy rules  after the revelations by former NSA  contractor Edward Snowden more than a  decade later  people in Europe were pretty upset about  the allegations that their data hosted  by American tech companies could have  been accessed by the NSA or other  intelligence services and a top EU Court  actually struck down that Safe Harbor  agreement and we've been seeing this  kind of back and forth between where to  put the line between  privacy and commerce going back all this  time and so most recently a new  agreement was struck down and companies  have been kind of operating in limbo are  they allowed to send data about people  living in Europe like me to servers in  the US so what does this executive order  mean for the data of people living in  the EU well this executive order  basically the restriction on sending  data is a European restriction Europe  says you can't send data outside of the  EU unless it's safe to do so and they  pre-authorize places that are safe so  Europe's ask was make it safe in  compliance with this court order that  struck down the prior agreement this is  the U.S executive order that that seeks  to do that based on negotiations with  the European commission the reason that  this agreement that allowed the data to  be sent was struck down was that an EU  Court said that Europeans didn't have  really actionable rights to do anything  about the surveillance if they were  thought they were being improperly  surveilled and that there needed to be  more legal redress and so what this new  agreement includes is on one hand new  guidelines and guard rails against what  the EU and U.S would call  disproportionate surveillance  surveillance that might say Seek to  repress dissent and then also this  redress that the EU Court had asked for  and it creates a new entity called the  data protection review court that could  give a secondary review to cases if  Europeans complain in the EU their  complaints can be sent to this court for  a kind of adjudication eventually so  what happens now that this executive  order has been signed well in the EU the  executive arm called the European  commission is going to review the  executive order and see if it matches  what they've been asking for  both the U.S and the commission really  want to make sure that this survives  what will be an inevitable Court review  and it's going to go back to the same  court that struck down the prior  agreement so both the European  commission and the U.S want to make sure  this deal survives a court review and  that's why they negotiated it this way  you know an EU official on Friday said  that they were confident that they could  defend this new data agreement against  legal challenges which suggests that  they're likely to give the executive  order that rubber stamp approval called  adequacy which will allow companies to  send their data more freely from the EU  to the US  all right what does this mean though for  tech companies and particularly for some  of the biggest U.S tech companies in  essence this helps remove some of the  uncertainty that they're facing about  the future of these data transfers after  the last court decision there have been  some cases moving through with EU  privacy Regulators that could have had a  really severe impact on for instance  meta there's a pending order  that meta should stop both Facebook and  Instagram from sending any data about  Europeans to the US that's still going  to take a few months to come through and  it may in fact come through before this  deal is finalized meta has said that if  that order were made effective that they  might have to stop offering some of  their services in Europe so it's a  significant operational challenge for a  company like that  there's a lot of other companies that  use American cloud services and if this  kind of data transfer were ruled illegal  would have to perhaps start storing more  data in Europe and so it is a  significant relief for a lot of  companies to see that this deal is  coming to fruition and it seems more  likely to be finally approved okay we'll  have to leave it there that's our  European Tech reporter Sam schechner  thanks for joining us Sam always a  pleasure  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com and if you  like our show please rate and review it  you can do that wherever you get your  podcasts I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall  Street Journal  thanks for listening  thank you  foreign

